  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and cool tonight with rain showers and isolated \nthunderstorms arriving Saturday night and Sunday.\n\nHigh pressure continues to slide across southern Canada \npromoting dry weather across western and north-central NY today \nand most of Saturday. Portions of north-central NY near the Tug \nHill and the Black River Valley may see temperatures fall into \nthe upper 30s by Saturday morning as we are only ~2 weeks away \nfrom the average first fall freeze for those locations. \n\nThe next round of precipitation across western NY will arrive \nSaturday night continuing into Sunday as a frontal boundary settles \nacross the midwest extending eastward towards PA. Isentropic lift \nalong with southerly moisture advection into the frontal boundary \nwill support rain shower development Saturday night. Additionally, \nmultiple shortwave troughs look to pass over the frontal boundary \ncontinuing through Sunday before the large scale trough axis in \neastern Canada passes through and broad high pressure builds in once \nagain across southern Canada early next week. With the frontal \nboundary primarily remaining south of the CWA, instability will be \nlimited for the development of deeper convective cores. However, \nperiods of heavy rainfall cannot be ruled out as PWAT values around \n1.5 inches sits above the climatological 90th percentile for mid-\nSeptember and the presence of warm cloud layer depth around 10-12 \nkft. The latest NBM shows probabilities of exceeding 1 inch of \nrainfall around 60%, mainly across the Southern Tier, for Saturday \nnight through Sunday.",
